Hello,

I'm trying to set up a Debian system that can fully upgrade automatically. I know this can have unwanted effects, but it's not a production machine and it's not a problem for my use-case (I can just reinstall the machine if it is faulty).

I used these APT settings:

```perl
# options for apt.systemd.daily
APT::Periodic::Enable "1";
APT::Periodic::Verbose "2";
APT::Periodic::Update-Package-Lists "always";
APT::Periodic::Download-Upgradeable-Packages "always";
APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade "always";
APT::Periodic::AutocleanInterval "always";

# allow upgrades between major versions (not recommended by the Debian project)
Acquire::AllowReleaseInfoChange::Suite "true";
Acquire::AllowReleaseInfoChange::Origin "true";
Acquire::AllowReleaseInfoChange::Label "true";
Acquire::AllowReleaseInfoChange::Version "true";
Acquire::AllowReleaseInfoChange::Codename "true";

# options for unattended-upgrades
Unattended-Upgrade::Origins-Pattern {
	"o=*,a=*";
};
Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
};
Unattended-Upgrade::AutoFixInterruptedDpkg "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Remove-Unused-Kernel-Packages "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Remove-New-Unused-Dependencies "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Remove-Unused-Dependencies "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot-WithUsers "true";
```

Unfortunately, sometimes, packages need to be deleted to upgrade some other packages, but unattended-upgrades explicitly prevents this:

```python
def sanity_problem(cache, desired_pkg):
    # ...
    changes = cache.get_changes()
    for pkg in changes:
        if pkg.marked_delete:
            return ("pkg %s is marked to be deleted" % pkg.name)
    # ...
    return None
```

I commented out the check for `pkg.marked_delete` and it has run successfully, but I have been blocked by packages requiring manual upgrades due to conffiles, so I've also had to disable the check `if conffile_prompt(item.destfile):`.

Is there a way to add options to allow deletion of packages and allow upgrades of packages with conffiles? (Apparently it defaults to installing new versions of configuration files, but I'm not completely sure, in my case it wasn't a configuration file I edited myself - hopefully it won't overwrite the APT configuration files...)

I can contribute to this, but it's possible that it's more complicated than it seems.

Also, I'm wondering if I should just replace unattended-upgrades with a much simpler script that runs `apt-get` non-interactively, with the adequate commands and options, then reboot if needed.

I'm not sure if this has already been done, so sorry if it has...

One of the problems with Windows back in the NHS has always been lack of Updating, and I fear something similar could happen with NHSbuntu, unless, you set up Unattended Upgrades in the distribution. *This Can Be Set Up To Work Before The OS Is Made Into An ISO*

To set Unattended Upgrades up:
In Terminal, run this:
`sudo apt-get install unattended-upgrades -y` (-y assumes YES for all possible prompts)
Now, go to '/etc/apt/apt.conf.d' in something that's got Superuser Privileges, and make sure a file that's EXACTLY called '50unattended-upgrades' exists, if not, make a blank document and name it to that, without the quotes.

Open it in your favourite text editor, and configure it something like how I did it in feren OS (below):
